  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Towards long-term Sustainable Change for Street Children (Kenya)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nyanza province in Kenya has a HIV prevalence rate (15.3%) that is double the national figure. The impact of HIV and AIDS and the 2008 post-election violence have resulted in a high number of children from Nyanza and neighbouring provinces moving to the streets for their survival. Once on the street the children lack food, basic health care, education and safe place to sleep at night; often they are forced to take up dangerous work and are frequently abused, raped and harassed by community members, police and older children; significant number of girls&apos; experience of sexual abuse as domestic maids pushed them on the street. This project will reach out to children on the street; run a drop in centre meeting their immediate needs and priorities such as washing, basic health care, counselling and non-formal education with separate facilities for boys and girls; provide short term accommodation pending reintegration with their families or guardians; strengthen Consortium of Street Children as a platform for doing advocacy work influencing policies. This project has a strong focus on building HOVIC&apos;s capacity including governance and decision making to deliver effective but complex projects supporting vulnerable boys and girls to achieve long term lasting change in their lives towards independent adulthood.</narrative>
  </description>
